---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { skanpayDefinitions } from "../content/site";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="SkanPay - Payment and Redemption"
  description="SkanPay helps participating wallets, merchant checkout, and payment partners coordinate eligible value, final balance, and confirmation in one checkout flow."
  activePath="/qr-payments/"
>
  <section class="tech-hero">
    <div class="tech-hero-inner">
      <div class="tech-hero-copy motion-item">
        <p class="eyebrow">SkanPay</p>
        <h1>One flow for offers, checkout, and payment.</h1>
        <p>
          SkanPay is the broader product direction behind BeamCash: a patent-pending payment-and-redemption platform for participating systems that need eligible value applied, final balance paid, and results confirmed in one checkout flow.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="request-object motion-item" aria-label="SkanPay checkout outcome snapshot">
        <div><span>Customer</span><strong>Uses a participating wallet or value source</strong></div>
        <div><span>Merchant</span><strong>Keeps checkout and invoice authority</strong></div>
        <div><span>Value</span><strong>Eligible coupon, voucher, gift card, or loyalty benefit</strong></div>
        <div><span>Payment</span><strong>Final balance moves through the supported path</strong></div>
        <div><span>Result</span><strong>Authorized parties receive confirmation</strong></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="definition-section">
    <div class="definition-inner">
      <div class="tech-section-heading motion-item">
        <p class="eyebrow">Definition</p>
        <h2>A practical layer for one coordinated checkout.</h2>
        <p>SkanPay should be understood by the problem it solves, not by the transport method. QR can be one way to start the flow, but the product value is the coordinated checkout outcome.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="definition-lines" aria-label="What SkanPay is and is not">
        {skanpayDefinitions.map((group) => (
          <article class="motion-item">
            <h3>{group.label}</h3>
            <ul>
              {group.items.map((item) => (
                <li>{item}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="state-machine">
    <div class="state-machine-inner">
      <div class="tech-section-heading motion-item">
        <p class="eyebrow">Checkout flow</p>
        <h2>What the customer and merchant should experience.</h2>
        <p>The flow should feel simple even when several business systems participate behind the scenes.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="state-rail" aria-label="SkanPay checkout flow">
        <article class="motion-item">
          <span>Present</span>
          <strong>Customer presents value</strong>
          <p>A participating app, QR, barcode, NFC signal, or other supported method can begin the checkout flow.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="motion-item">
          <span>Apply</span>
          <strong>Eligible value is checked</strong>
          <p>The merchant checkout determines what coupon, voucher, gift-card, or loyalty value can apply.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="motion-item">
          <span>Pay</span>
          <strong>Final balance is paid</strong>
          <p>The checkout continues through the supported payment path after the eligible value is handled.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="motion-item">
          <span>Confirm</span>
          <strong>Results are confirmed</strong>
          <p>The right participants can receive confirmation of what was redeemed, charged, and completed.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="protocol-split">
    <div class="protocol-split-inner">
      <div class="protocol-copy motion-item">
        <p class="eyebrow">For partners</p>
        <h2>Start with one measurable checkout problem.</h2>
        <p>
          The best first use case is narrow: one wallet or value source, one participating merchant environment, one gateway path, and one redemption type with a clear success measure.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="boundary-map motion-item" aria-label="Gateway and partner responsibilities">
        <div>
          <span>Wallet or offer owner</span>
          <strong>Extend accepted value</strong>
          <p>Give customers a reason to use an existing account, coupon, voucher, gift card, or loyalty benefit.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span>Merchant or platform</span>
          <strong>Preserve checkout control</strong>
          <p>Keep invoice logic, eligibility, and merchant operations inside the systems that already own them.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span>Gateway path</span>
          <strong>Coordinate the handoff</strong>
          <p>Support a controlled flow without building a separate direct connection between every wallet and merchant.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="integration-runway">
    <div class="integration-runway-inner">
      <div class="tech-section-heading motion-item">
        <p class="eyebrow">Pilot scope</p>
        <h2>What we would define before a pilot.</h2>
        <p>Public messaging should stay clear and measured. The detailed operating model belongs in a partner review.</p>
      </div>

      <ol class="runway-list">
        <li class="motion-item"><span>01</span><strong>Participating wallet or value source</strong></li>
        <li class="motion-item"><span>02</span><strong>Merchant checkout environment</strong></li>
        <li class="motion-item"><span>03</span><strong>Coupon, voucher, gift-card, or loyalty rule</strong></li>
        <li class="motion-item"><span>04</span><strong>Confirmation and support states</strong></li>
        <li class="motion-item"><span>05</span><strong>Success measure and data boundaries</strong></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
